  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed approach uses gradient descent with projected updates to minimize the maximum overlap integral. It leverages the initial solution if available and systematically adjusts the function h to reduce the worst-case overlap across all shifts. This method addresses the high-dimensional search space and non-convexity of the problem through iterative improvement guided by gradient information.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Decision variable: h \u2208 [0, 1]^n with sum(h) = n/2\n- Objective: min h max\u2096 \u2211 h[j](1 - h[j+k]) * dx\n- Transformation: For shift k, overlap = sum(h * (1 - h_shifted)) * dx\n- Gradient: For active k, \u2207C = (1 - h_shifted) * dx\n- Projection: clip to [0, 1] and normalize to sum(h) = n/2\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization:\n   - Use initial_h_values if provided, ensuring feasibility.\n   - Random feasible initialization if none provided.\n\nb. Iterative optimization loop:\n   1. Compute cross-correlation for all shifts.\n   2. Identify shift(s) with maximum overlap.\n   3. Compute gradient for the active shift(s).\n   4. Update h with gradient descent step.\n   5. Project to feasible region:\n      i. Clip each element to [0, 1]\n      ii. Normalize sum to n/2 by scaling\n\nc. Termination:\n   - Stop after budget exhausted or no improvement.\n\nd. Best solution tracking:\n   - Maintain and return the best h and C\u2085 found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Learning rate: adaptive based on iterations\n- Gradient computation: numerical or analytical\n- Projection method: constant scaling or mass redistribution\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Time is spent in gradient computations and projections.\n- Budget is allocated to iterations, with robustness via projection enforcing feasibility.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Ensure feasibility of final h: 0 <= h[i] <= 1 and sum(h) = n/2.\n- Validate C\u2085 computation using the official evaluator.\n\nThe final procedure returns the best h, its C\u2085 bound, and the number of points used.",
